{
  "id": "hamming-distance",
  "questionId": "hamming-distance",
  "slug": "hamming-distance",
  "title": "Hamming Distance",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Bit manipulation",
  "patterns": [
    "bit manipulation",
    "xor"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "what single operation tells you, per bit, whether two bits are different?",
    "XOR gives you a 1 at every position where x and y differ, and a 0 where they agree",
    "so x XOR y directly encodes the set of differing positions as set bits",
    "compute z = x ^ y, then count the set bits of z using Brian Kernighan's z & (z - 1) trick (or your language's built-in popcount)"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "manually comparing bits one at a time with shifting when a single XOR plus popcount is simpler and less error-prone",
    "computing x - y or abs(x - y) instead of x XOR y, which measures something entirely different",
    "forgetting the distance for x == y should be 0"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given two integers `x` and `y`.\n\n**Return the Hamming distance between them, the number of bit positions at which their binary representations differ.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= x, y <= 2^31 - 1"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "x and y are equal (distance 0)",
      "x and y share no set bits at all",
      "one of x or y is 0"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
